"```python\nimport inspect\nimport funcsigs\n\n# Define a function signature\ndef my_function(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is a function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a + b + c\n\n# Define a function with default values\ndef my_function_with_default(a=1, b=2, c=3):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function. Defaults to 1.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function. Defaults to 'hello'.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function. Defaults to 4.0.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a + b + c\n\n# Define a function with keyword-only arguments\ndef my_function_with_kwargs(a, b, c, *, d, e):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function. Must not be 'x'.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function. Must not be 'y'.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function. Must not be 'z'.\n    d (int): The fourth parameter of the function.\n    e (str): The fifth parameter of the function.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a + b + c + d + e\n\n# Define a function with named arguments\ndef my_function_with_named_args(a, b, c, d, e, f):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function. Must be 'x'.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function. Must be 'y'.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function. Must be 'z'.\n    d (int): The fourth parameter of the function. Must be 'w'.\n    e (str): The fifth parameter of the function. Must be 'v'.\n    f (float): The sixth parameter of the function. Must be 'u'.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a + b + c + d + e + f\n\n# Define a function with positional-only arguments\ndef my_function_with_positional_only(a, b, c, d, e):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function. Must not be 'x'.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function. Must not be 'y'.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function. Must not be 'z'.\n    d (int): The fourth parameter of the function. Must not be 'w'.\n    e (str): The fifth parameter of the function. Must not be 'v'.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a + b + c + d + e\n\n# Define a function with a single return value\ndef my_function_with_single_return_value(a, b, c):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a + b + c\n\n# Define a function with multiple return values\ndef my_function_with_multiple_return_values(a, b, c, d, e):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function.\n    d (int): The fourth parameter of the function.\n    e (str): The fifth parameter of the function.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a, b, c, d, e\n\n# Define a function with keyword-only return value\ndef my_function_with_keyword_only_return_value(a, b, c, *, d, e):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function. Must not be 'x'.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function. Must not be 'y'.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function. Must not be 'z'.\n    d (int): The fourth parameter of the function.\n    e (str): The fifth parameter of the function. Must not be 'v'.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a, b, c, d, e\n\n# Define a function with named return value\ndef my_function_with_named_return_value(a, b, c, d, e, f):\n    \"\"\"\n    This is another function signature as per PEP362.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    a (int): The first parameter of the function. Must be 'x'.\n    b (str): The second parameter of the function. Must be 'y'.\n    c (float): The third parameter of the function. Must be 'z'.\n    d (int): The fourth parameter of the function. Must be 'w'.\n    e (str): The fifth parameter of the function. Must be 'v'.\n    f (float): The sixth parameter of the function. Must be 'u'.\n    \"\"\"\n    return a, b, c, d, e, f\n```"
"```python\nfrom flask import Flask\nfrom prometheus_client import Counter, Gauge, Histogram, start_http_server, GaugeRegistry, CounterRegistry, HistogramRegistry\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# Create a registry to store metrics\nmetric_registry = GaugeRegistry()\ncounter_registry = CounterRegistry()\nhistogram_registry = HistogramRegistry()\n\n# Define metrics for the application\n@app.before_request\ndef before_request():\n    # Create a metric to track the number of requests\n    metric_registry.gauge('requests_count', 'Number of requests received')\n    counter_registry.counter('requests_count', 'Number of requests received')\n\n@app.after_request\ndef after_request(response):\n    # Create a metric to track the response time\n    response_time = gauge('response_time', 'Response time in seconds')\n    histogram_registry.burst('response_time', 'Response time in seconds')\n    return response with response_time and histogram_registry.burst('response_time', 'Response time in seconds')\n\n# Define a function to start the Prometheus server\ndef start_prometheus():\n    start_http_server(8000)\n    metric_registry.start_http_server()\n    counter_registry.start_http_server()\n    histogram_registry.start_http_server()\n\n# Start the Flask application and Prometheus server\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    start_prometheus()\n```"
"```python\nimport uvloop\n\nasync def main():\n    \"\"\"Create an asynchronous event loop using libuv.\"\"\"\n    loop = uvloop.new_event_loop()\n    try:\n        await loop.run_forever()\n    finally:\n        loop.close()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"
"```python\nimport asyncio\nimport unittest\n\nclass TestAsyncUnitTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    async def test_async_test_case(self):\n        # Create a task that sleeps for 1 second\n        await asyncio.sleep(1)\n\n        # Create another task that sleeps for 2 seconds\n        await asyncio.sleep(2)\n\n        # Run the tasks concurrently\n        await asyncio.gather(1, 2, 3)\n\n    def test_error_handling(self):\n        # Try to run an async test case with a non-async function\n        try:\n            async def non_async_function():\n                await asyncio.sleep(1)\n        except asyncio.CancelledError:\n            self.fail(\"CancelledError was raised\")\n\n        # Try to run an async test case with a function that raises an exception\n        try:\n            async def async_function():\n                raise asyncio CancelledError()\n        except asyncio.CancelledError:\n            self.fail(\"CancelledError was raised\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\nThis code enhances the standard `unittest` package by adding support for testing asynchronous functions using the `asyncio` library. It provides two test cases: `test_async_test_case` and `test_error_handling`. The `test_async_test_case` test case runs two asynchronous tasks concurrently and checks if the tests pass. The `test_error_handling` test case tries to run an async test case with a non-async function and a function that raises an exception, and checks if the `unittest.main()` function raises an `asyncio.CancelledError` as expected."
