"```python\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\ndef parse_iso_date(date_string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Parse an ISO 8601 date string into a Python datetime object.\n\n    Args:\n        date_string (str): The ISO 8601 date string to parse.\n\n    Returns:\n        datetime: The parsed datetime object.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        return datetime.fromisoformat(date_string)\n    except ValueError:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid ISO 8601 date string\")\n\n# Example usage:\ndate_string = \"2022-07-25T16:00:00\"\nparsed_date = parse_iso_date(date_string)\nprint(parsed_date)  # Output: 2022-07-25 16:00:00\n```"
"To create a Python application that can render various types of content to the terminal, you'll need to install the required libraries. Here's a step-by-step guide:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport time\nimport datetime\nimport platform\nimport subprocess\nimport terminal\nimport tabulate\nimport markdown\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Setting up the environment\nif platform.system() == 'Windows':\n    os.environ['PATH'] = os.path.insert(os.path.split(os.path.splitdrive(os.getcwd())[0])[0], r'\\C:\\Python3x\\bin')\nelse:\n    os.environ['PATH'] = os.path.insert(os.path.split(os.path.splitdrive(os.getcwd())[0])[0], r'\\usr/bin')\n```\n\n```python\ndef render_to_terminal(data, title, style=None, font_size=12):\n    # Markdown rendering\n    if style is None:\n        style = 'default'\n    if style == 'rich':\n        # Rich text rendering\n        print(f\"{title}\\n{markdown.markdown(data, style=style)}\\n\")\n    elif style == 'table':\n        # Table rendering\n        table_data = [[f\"{cell}\"] * len(data[0]) for cell in data[0]]\n        print(f\"{title}\\n{tabulate(table_data, headers=['Cells'], tablefmt=style)}\\n\")\n    elif style == 'code':\n        # Code rendering\n        print(f\"{title}\\n{terminal.print(f'```python\\n{markdown.markdown(data, style=style)}\\n```')}\\n\")\n    elif style == 'highlight':\n        # Syntax highlighting\n        print(f\"{title}\\n{terminal.print(f'```python\\n{markdown.markdown(data, style=style)}\\n```')}\\n\")\n    else:\n        # Default rendering\n        print(f\"{title}\\n{markdown.markdown(data, style='default')}\\n\")\n```\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    data = {\n        'Title': ['Table of Contents', 'Table', 'Code', 'Syntax Highlighting'],\n        'Data': [\n            ['Item 1', 'Item 2', 'Item 3', 'Item 4'],\n            ['Item 5', 'Item 6', 'Item 7', 'Item 8'],\n            ['Item 9', 'Item 10', 'Item 11', 'Item 12'],\n            ['Item 13', 'Item 14', 'Item 15', 'Item 16']\n        ]\n    }\n\n    title = \"My Terminal Application\"\n    style = 'rich'\n\n    while True:\n        print(\"\\nTerminal Application\")\n        print(\"-------------------------------\")\n        print(f\"Title: {title}\")\n        print(f\"Style: {style}\")\n        print(\"-------------------------------\")\n        print(render_to_terminal(data, title, style, 12))\n        time.sleep(1)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```"
"```python\nimport typing\n\nclass RuntimeTypeChecker:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # Initialize a dictionary to store the type of each variable\n        self.variables = {}\n\n    def check_type(self, var_name, var_value):\n        \"\"\"\n        Check if the variable is of the correct type at runtime.\n\n        Args:\n            var_name (str): The name of the variable to check.\n            var_value: The value of the variable to check.\n\n        Raises:\n            TypeError: If the variable is not of the correct type.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Get the type of the variable\n        var_type = typing.get_type(var_value)\n\n        # Check if the variable is already in the dictionary\n        if var_name in self.variables:\n            # If the variable is already in the dictionary, check if its type is correct\n            if self.variables[var_name] != var_type:\n                raise TypeError(f\"Variable '{var_name}' is of type {self.variables[var_name]}, but should be of type {var_type}\")\n        else:\n            # If the variable is not in the dictionary, add it with its type\n            self.variables[var_name] = var_type\n\n    def run(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Run the type checker and raise errors if any type mismatches are found.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Iterate over all variables in the dictionary\n        for var_name, var_type in self.variables.items():\n            # Check if the variable is of the correct type\n            self.check_type(var_name, var_type)\n\n\n# Example usage:\nchecker = RuntimeTypeChecker()\nchecker.run()\n```\n\nThis code implements a simple run-time type checker for Python, using the `typing` module to check the types of variables at runtime. The `RuntimeTypeChecker` class has a `check_type` method that takes a variable name and its value as input, and raises a `TypeError` if the variable is not of the correct type. The `run` method runs the type checker and raises errors if any type mismatches are found."
