"```python\nimport dask.dataframe as dd\n\n# Define a sample function to be scheduled\ndef sample_function(x):\n    # Simulate some work\n    import time\n    time.sleep(1)\n    return x * x\n\n# Create a Dask DataFrame from the sample function\ndf = dd.from_array([sample_function(i) for i in range(10)], npartitions=4)\n\n# Schedule the DataFrame for parallel execution with retries\ntry:\n    # Try to schedule the DataFrame\n    df_scheduled = df.sched()\n    # Wait for the scheduled tasks to complete\n    df_scheduled.compute()\nexcept dask.distributed.ClientError as e:\n    # Handle any errors that occur during scheduling\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\nfinally:\n    # Clean up\n    df_scheduled.compute()\n```\n\nIn this code:\n\n1.  We import the `dask.dataframe` module, which is used to create Dask DataFrames.\n2.  We define a sample function `sample_function` that takes an integer `x` as input and returns its square.\n3.  We create a Dask DataFrame `df` from the sample function using `dd.from_array`.\n4.  We schedule the Dask DataFrame `df` for parallel execution across multiple workers using `df.sched()`. We specify the number of partitions (4 in this case) to distribute the work across multiple workers.\n5.  We wait for the scheduled tasks to complete using `compute()`. If any errors occur during scheduling, we catch the `ClientError` exception and print an error message.\n6.  Finally, we clean up by computing the scheduled tasks."
