"Here's an example of a Flask-OAuthlib server and client for OAuth and OpenID Connect:\n\n**Server Code (server.py)**\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\nfrom flask_oauthlib.client import OAuth2Client\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# OAuth2 Client Settings\nCLIENT_ID = 'your_client_id'\nCLIENT_SECRET = 'your_client_secret'\nAUTHORIZE_URL = 'https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth'\nTOKEN_URL = 'https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/token'\nUSERINFO_URL = 'https://www.googleapis.com/identity/userinfo/v1/userinfo'\n\n# Authorization Code Flow\ndef get_authorization_code():\n    return request.args.get('code')\n\n# Token Request\ndef get_token(code):\n    params = {\n        'code': code,\n        'redirect_uri': 'http://localhost:5000/callback',\n        'grant_type': 'authorization_code',\n        'client_id': CLIENT_ID,\n        'client_secret': CLIENT_SECRET,\n        'scope': 'openid profile email'\n    }\n    return requests.post(TOKEN_URL, params=params)\n\n# User Info Request\ndef get_user_info(token):\n    headers = {\n        'Authorization': f'Bearer {token}',\n        'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'\n    }\n    response = requests.get(USERINFO_URL, headers=headers)\n    return response.json()\n\n# Callback Handler\n@app.route('/callback', methods=['GET'])\ndef callback():\n    code = request.args.get('code')\n    token = get_token(code)\n    user_info = get_user_info(token)\n    return jsonify(user_info)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    app.run(debug=True)\n```\n\n**Client Code (client.py)**\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, request, jsonify\nfrom flask_oauthlib.client import OAuth2Client\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n# OAuth2 Client Settings\nCLIENT_ID = 'your_client_id'\nCLIENT_SECRET = 'your_client_secret'\nAUTHORIZE_URL = 'https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth'\nTOKEN_URL = 'https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/token'\nUSERINFO_URL = 'https://www.googleapis.com/identity/userinfo/v1/userinfo'\n\n# Authorization Code Flow\ndef get_authorization_code():\n    return request.args.get('code')\n\n# Token Request\ndef get_token(code):\n    params = {\n        'code': code,\n        'redirect_uri': 'http://localhost:5000/callback',\n        'grant_type': 'authorization_code',\n        'client_id': CLIENT_ID,\n        'client_secret': CLIENT_SECRET,\n        'scope': 'openid profile email'\n    }\n    return requests.post(TOKEN_URL, params=params)\n\n# User Info Request\ndef get_user_info(token):\n    headers = {\n        'Authorization': f'Bearer {token}',\n        'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'\n    }\n    response = requests.get(USERINFO_URL, headers=headers)\n    return response.json()\n\n# Redirect Handler\n@app.route('/authorize', methods=['GET'])\ndef authorize():\n    code = get_authorization_code()\n    token_response = get_token(code)\n    user_info_response = get_user_info(token_response['access_token'])\n    return jsonify(user_info_response)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    app.run(debug=True)\n```\n\n**Usage**\n\n1. Run the server by executing `python server.py`.\n2. Open a web browser and navigate to `http://localhost:5000/authorize` after authorization.\n3. Select the desired OAuth2 provider (e.g. Google).\n4. Follow the prompts to redirect back to the server after authorization.\n5. The server will redirect back to the client with an authorization code.\n6. The client can use the authorization code to obtain an access token by executing `python client.py`.\n7. The client can use the access token to obtain user information by executing `python client.py userInfo`.\n\nNote: This is a basic example and you should consider implementing additional security measures such as token blacklisting and refresh tokens."
"```python\nimport schedule\nimport time\n\ndef schedule_function(func, interval, cron_string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates a scheduling function that runs a given function at a specified interval.\n    \n    Args:\n        func (function): The function to be executed.\n        interval (int): The interval at which the function should be executed.\n        cron_string (str): A cron-like string representing the timing of the execution.\n        \n    Returns:\n        None\n    \"\"\"\n    # Define a function to be executed\n    def execute_func():\n        func()\n\n    # Schedule the function to be executed at the specified interval\n    schedule.every(interval).seconds.do(execute_func)\n\n    # Loop indefinitely\n    while True:\n        # Run the scheduled tasks\n        schedule.run_pending()\n        # Wait for one second before checking again\n        time.sleep(1)\n```\n\nExample use case:\n\n```python\n# Define a function to be executed\ndef hello_world():\n    print(\"Hello, World!\")\n\n# Schedule the function to be executed every 5 seconds\nschedule_function(hello_world, 5, \"0 0 * * ?\")  # Run at midnight, every day\n```\n\nThis code creates a scheduling function that takes a function to be executed, an interval at which to run it, and a cron-like string representing the timing of the execution. The function is then scheduled to be executed at the specified interval using the `schedule` module."
"```python\n# cython: language_level=3\n\n# Define a function to allocate memory using calloc\ndef allocate_memory(size):\n    \"\"\"\n    Allocates memory using calloc and returns a pointer to it.\n    \n    Args:\n        size (int): The size of the memory to allocate.\n    \n    Returns:\n        CData: A pointer to the allocated memory.\n    \"\"\"\n    import ctypes\n    return ctypes.c_void_p(calloc(size))\n\n# Define a function to deallocate memory using free\ndef deallocate_memory(ptr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Deallocates memory using free and releases any resources it may hold.\n    \n    Args:\n        ptr (CData): A pointer to the memory to deallocate.\n    \n    Returns:\n        None\n    \"\"\"\n    free(ptr)\n\n# Define a function to wrap calls to calloc and free\ndef memory_manager(size):\n    \"\"\"\n    A wrapper function for calloc and free, compatible with both Python and C.\n    \n    Args:\n        size (int): The size of the memory to allocate.\n    \n    Returns:\n        CData: A pointer to the allocated memory.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Allocate memory using calloc\n    ptr = allocate_memory(size)\n    \n    # Deallocate memory using free\n    deallocate_memory(ptr)\n    \n    # Return the pointer to the allocated memory\n    return ptr\n```"
